{"name":"Happy Trails","hex":"#b67a63","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"happy trails","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of happy trail"]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["An expression of good wishes for somebody departing for a voyage or for an indefinite period of time: best wishes, good luck."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/happy_trails"}],"entries":[{"title":"Happy Trails (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Happy Trails\" is a country song performed by Roy Rogers and his wife Dale Evans and written by Evans. It is known as the theme song for both the radio and television versions of The Roy Rogers Show, in which they also starred. It was written by Evans and performed during the outros of the shows. Evans wrote \"Happy Trails\" after deciding that the shows needed a outro song. Prior to the composition of \"Happy Trails\", the song \"Smiles Are Made of Sunshine\" was used, also by Rogers and Evans."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happy_Trails_(song)"},{"title":"Happy Trails (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Happy Trails is the second album of the American band Quicksilver Messenger Service. Most of the album was recorded from two performances at the Fillmore East and Fillmore West, although it is not clear which parts were recorded at which Fillmore. The record was released by Capitol Records in 1969 in stereo."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/58/Quicksilver_Messenger_Service-Happy_Trails_%28album_cover%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happy_Trails_(album)"},{"title":"Happiness","blocks":[{"text":"Happiness is a complex and multifaceted mental state that encompasses a range of positive feelings, from contentment to intense joy. It is often associated with positive life experiences, such as achieving goals, spending time with loved ones, or engaging in enjoyable activities. However, happiness can also arise spontaneously, without any apparent external cause."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Girl_of_Vietnam.jpg/330px-Girl_of_Vietnam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happiness"},{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Sweet_Baby_Kisses_Family_Love.jpg/500px-Sweet_Baby_Kisses_Family_Love.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Sweet_Baby_Kisses_Family_Love.jpg/1280px-Sweet_Baby_Kisses_Family_Love.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Sweet_Baby_Kisses_Family_Love.jpg/500px-Sweet_Baby_Kisses_Family_Love.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Woman kissing a baby on the cheek","link":"https://en.wikipedia.o